<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-intervals-c-major-scale" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>C Major Scale Construction</title>

  <p>In C major, the interval pattern remains the same ascending and descending.</p>
  <p>All major scales have the same pattern of intervals, so any major scale can be built by using this same pattern.</p>
  <p>The pattern of intervals in a major scale is: whole, whole, half, whole, whole, whole, half. This means that starting from the root note (C in this case), you would move up a whole step to D, then another whole step to E, then a half step to F, then a whole step to G, then a whole step to A, then a whole step to B, and finally a half step back to C.</p>
  <p> A whole step (also called a whole tone) consists of two semitones, while a half step (also called a semitone) consists of one semitone. So the interval pattern for the C major scale can also be expressed in terms of semitones as: 2, 2, 1, 2, 2, 2, 1.</p>
  <p>The C major scale notes are C, D, E, F, G, A, B, and C.</p>

  <figure xml:id="fig-chromatic-scale-clock">
    <caption>The 12 notes of the chromatic scale arranged clockwise around a circle, provide a useful means of visualising how scales are constructed by stacking 2nd intervals above the Scale 
      Root note.</caption>
    <image>
      <latex-image>
        <![CDATA[
\begin{tikzpicture}
  \def\R{2.0}
  \def\LR{2.7}
  \def\TR{1.7}

  \draw (0,0) circle (\R cm);

  \foreach \i in {0,...,11}{
    \pgfmathsetmacro{\a}{90 - \i*30}
    \draw[thin] (\a:\TR cm) -- (\a:\R cm);
  }

  \node[font=\normalsize] at ( 90:\LR cm) {C};
  \node[font=\small] at ( 60:\LR cm) {C$\sharp$/D$\flat$};
  \node[font=\normalsize] at ( 30:\LR cm) {D};
  \node[font=\small] at (  0:\LR cm) {D$\sharp$/E$\flat$};
  \node[font=\normalsize] at (-30:\LR cm) {E};
  \node[font=\normalsize] at (-60:\LR cm) {F};
  \node[font=\small] at (-90:\LR cm) {F$\sharp$/G$\flat$};
  \node[font=\normalsize] at (-120:\LR cm) {G};
  \node[font=\small] at (-150:\LR cm) {G$\sharp$/A$\flat$};
  \node[font=\normalsize] at ( 180:\LR cm) {A};
  \node[font=\small] at ( 150:\LR cm) {A$\sharp$/B$\flat$};
  \node[font=\normalsize] at ( 120:\LR cm) {B};
\end{tikzpicture}
        ]]>
      </latex-image>
    </image>
  </figure>

  <figure xml:id="fig-c-major-scale-notation">
    <caption>C major scale notation on a musical staff (ascending and descending).</caption>
    <image>
      <latex-image>
        <![CDATA[
\begin{music}
  \nobarnumbers
  \setclef10
  \startextract
  \notes \qa c \qa d \qa e \qa f \qa g \qa h \qa i \qa j \qa i \qa h \qa g \qa f \qa e \qa d \qa c \en
  \endextract
\end{music}
        ]]>
      </latex-image>
    </image>
  </figure>

  <figure xml:id="fig-intervals-c-major-scale-table">
    <caption>Examples of intervals with notation and semitone counts (C major scale).</caption>
    <image>
      <latex-image>
        <![CDATA[
\newcommand{\MusiC}[1]{\adjustbox{trim=145pt 0pt 140pt 0pt, valign=c}{%
  \begin{music}
    \nobarnumbers
    \setclef10
    \startextract
    \notes #1
    \endextract
  \end{music}}}

\begin{tabular}{|c|c|c|c|c|c|}
  \hline
  Interval & Quality & Notes & On Staff & Number of Semitones & Count\\
  \hline
  Second & Major & C~-~D & \MusiC{\qa c \qa d \en} & 2 & 3\\
  \hline
  Second & Major & D~-~E & \MusiC{\qa d \qa e \en} & 2 & 3\\
  \hline
  Second & Minor & E~-~F & \MusiC{\qa e \qa f \en} & 1 & 2\\
  \hline
  Second & Major & F~-~G & \MusiC{\qa f \qa g \en} & 2 & 3\\
  \hline
  Second & Major & G~-~A & \MusiC{\qa g \qa h \en} & 2 & 3\\
  \hline
  Second & Major & A~-~B & \MusiC{\qa h \qa i \en} & 2 & 3\\
  \hline
  Second & Minor & B~-~C & \MusiC{\qa i \qa j \en} & 1 & 2\\
  \hline
\end{tabular}
        ]]>
      </latex-image>
    </image>
  </figure>

</section>
